1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It powers our jets and coats our lips.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
We drive on it, ignite it, and even play with it.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:14,000
And from the shirts on our backs to the roofs over our heads,

4
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:16,000
we depend on it.

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
So if you think crude is all about the pump, well, think again.

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Now, the Secrets of Oil on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,720
You've heard the warning.

8
00:00:41,640 --> 00:00:44,000
The world as we know it has come to an end.

9
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:49,000
Uncertain supplies and rising demand

10
00:00:49,001 --> 00:00:51,580
have made cheap oil a thing of the past.

11
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:56,060
Sure, that means higher prices at the gas pump,

12
00:00:56,440 --> 00:00:57,980
but that's not the only impact.

13
00:00:58,620 --> 00:00:59,700
Not by a long shot.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,540
You may not realize it, but the first secret of oil is simple. It's everywhere.

15
00:01:05,541 --> 00:01:19,541
We can talk about asphalt to aspirin, cosmetics to computers, helmets to heart valves, safety glass to shower curtains, umbrellas to ziplock bags and everything in

16
00:01:19,876 --> 00:01:20,580
between.

17
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:25,880
Oil lubricates our machines, our weapons, and even our skin.

18
00:01:25,881 --> 00:01:30,020
It binds the building materials in our roads and homes.

19
00:01:30,940 --> 00:01:36,140
And it provides the chemical building blocks for plastics, rubber, and synthetic fibers.

20
00:01:37,220 --> 00:01:37,980
Just look around.

21
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:43,920
30% of your television is made from petroleum-based materials.

22
00:01:44,460 --> 00:01:45,940
At least 50% of your sneakers.

23
00:01:47,020 --> 00:01:50,180
Your exercise attire, nearly 100% oil-derived.

24
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:53,020
And also the laundry detergent used to clean it.

25
00:01:54,020 --> 00:01:55,660
Contact lenses, check.

26
00:01:55,880 --> 00:01:57,880
Your toothbrush. Ditto.

27
00:01:57,881 --> 00:02:01,880
But wait, did he really say aspirin is made from oil?

28
00:02:01,881 --> 00:02:06,880
Aspirin and many other pharmaceuticals are petroleum-based products.

29
00:02:06,881 --> 00:02:08,880
Cancer-fighting drugs.

30
00:02:08,881 --> 00:02:11,880
I think it's an unknown fact that the petrochemical industry

31
00:02:11,881 --> 00:02:14,880
provides the basic building blocks.

32
00:02:14,881 --> 00:02:18,880
A little more than a century ago, none of this was true.

33
00:02:18,881 --> 00:02:21,880
Back then, rubber came from trees.

34
00:02:21,881 --> 00:02:23,880
Plastics were all but unknown.

35
00:02:23,881 --> 00:02:28,880
And our biggest mode of transportation required moving tons of coal.

36
00:02:28,881 --> 00:02:31,880
But then rose the combustion engine.

37
00:02:31,881 --> 00:02:35,880
Our mobility soared, and oil has been king ever since.

38
00:02:35,881 --> 00:02:42,880
Our whole society has really matured around the concept of this fantastic material available

39
00:02:42,881 --> 00:02:45,880
at a very low cost that could be delivered to our doors.

40
00:02:45,881 --> 00:02:50,880
And the last 100 years, 50 years, has been just figuring out new ways to take advantage

41
00:02:50,881 --> 00:02:54,860
of that great raw material, natural gas, crude oil,

42
00:02:55,300 --> 00:02:59,100
just making so many magical things happen.

43
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:03,300
A single barrel of crude equals 42 gallons,

44
00:03:04,180 --> 00:03:07,060
and Americans consume about 20 million of them every day.

45
00:03:09,680 --> 00:03:12,960
Roughly 50% of every barrel goes to finished gasoline,

46
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:15,680
while another 15% to diesel.

47
00:03:15,681 --> 00:03:22,080
Remarkably, it's that remaining 35% that's become the backbone of our world.

48
00:03:23,240 --> 00:03:28,000
So what is it about petroleum that makes it so versatile, and for now so vital?

49
00:03:30,220 --> 00:03:34,280
Inside each barrel is a rich mix of hydrogen and carbon molecules,

50
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:38,000
formed in all sorts of combinations known as hydrocarbons.

51
00:03:39,120 --> 00:03:43,680
Some like C1, better known as methane, are relatively simple hydrocarbons

52
00:03:43,681 --> 00:03:45,880
and are considered light.

53
00:03:47,060 --> 00:03:49,540
Automotive fuels contain slightly longer chains,

54
00:03:50,020 --> 00:03:53,160
while others, such as the chemical structure of motor oil,

55
00:03:53,660 --> 00:03:57,300
contain heavy, complex chains of 50 or more carbon atoms.

56
00:04:00,180 --> 00:04:02,760
Separate these different molecules within the crude,

57
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:06,700
and you generate potent energy sources like gasoline and diesel.

58
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:11,880
Yet within what remains is a rich assortment of chemical building blocks,

59
00:04:11,881 --> 00:04:13,440
known as feedstocks.

60
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:17,680
It all depends on refining.

61
00:04:17,681 --> 00:04:21,680
What a refinery basically does is take the crude oil,

62
00:04:21,681 --> 00:04:25,680
rearrange molecules through various processes,

63
00:04:25,681 --> 00:04:29,680
some to make gasoline, some to make diesel, some to make jet fuel,

64
00:04:29,681 --> 00:04:33,680
but a lot to make the basic building blocks for many industries

65
00:04:33,681 --> 00:04:37,680
worldwide to use as finished products.

66
00:04:37,681 --> 00:04:41,680
Valero Oil is the largest oil refiner in North America.

67
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:44,520
Here at their Wilmington, California refinery,

68
00:04:45,180 --> 00:04:48,420
they process more than 135,000 barrels daily,

69
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:52,620
which arrive via tanker ship and are pumped ashore.

70
00:04:53,020 --> 00:04:56,120
This is a line that we receive crude off of the crude ships.

71
00:04:56,260 --> 00:04:57,800
The crude is brought in through this line.

72
00:04:57,920 --> 00:04:58,880
It's a 30-inch line.

73
00:04:59,100 --> 00:05:01,320
It goes into the tanks that you see behind us.

74
00:05:01,780 --> 00:05:05,760
We have four crude tanks, approximately 300,000 barrels each.

75
00:05:06,580 --> 00:05:09,560
Think of any refinery as a giant chemistry set

76
00:05:09,561 --> 00:05:14,200
performing four basic tasks, distillation, hydrocleaning,

77
00:05:14,760 --> 00:05:16,080
cracking, and blending.

78
00:05:18,980 --> 00:05:23,240
Each stage is designed to create more exact and more pure feedstocks.

79
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:29,920
Stage one is distillation, which separates the primary hydrocarbons from one another.

80
00:05:30,860 --> 00:05:33,520
Crude distillation is the first step in the refining process.

81
00:05:34,080 --> 00:05:38,520
This is where we take the raw crude oil that comes in from the crude tanks,

82
00:05:38,521 --> 00:05:40,620
and we begin our initial separation.

83
00:05:41,620 --> 00:05:44,520
A furnace connected to this tower begins to boil the oil

84
00:05:44,521 --> 00:05:46,640
to upwards of 700 degrees Fahrenheit.

85
00:05:48,300 --> 00:05:51,900
As the heat intensifies, different hydrocarbon molecules

86
00:05:51,901 --> 00:05:54,860
within the crude begin to vaporize at different temperatures.

87
00:05:56,380 --> 00:05:59,680
Now separated, these vapors rise within the tower,

88
00:06:00,220 --> 00:06:04,340
cool, condense, and are then drawn off at specific levels.

89
00:06:04,341 --> 00:06:11,660
The lightest hydrocarbons, like the liquid gases used to make aspirin, rise to the top,

90
00:06:12,340 --> 00:06:17,140
while the heavier ones used in motor oils and road materials remain toward the bottom.

91
00:06:18,620 --> 00:06:24,360
The result? The once singular crude has been separated into a variety of fractions.

92
00:06:25,640 --> 00:06:30,320
What we get in crude fractionation is these products that you see here.

93
00:06:30,321 --> 00:06:37,300
So starting with the lightest product, we have natural gas, which has to be stored under pressure, stored in this container.

94
00:06:37,860 --> 00:06:42,460
We have a light gasoline product, a heavier gasoline product called naphtha.

95
00:06:43,180 --> 00:06:46,480
We have jet fuel range and diesel range products.

96
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:49,060
We have gas oil range products.

97
00:06:49,920 --> 00:06:54,580
And we have residuals like asphalt, or in this case, this is a sample of coke.

98
00:06:55,920 --> 00:06:59,740
None of the fractions that come off the distillation tower are finished products.

99
00:07:00,320 --> 00:07:04,560
and all will require further processing.

100
00:07:04,561 --> 00:07:06,940
After more than a million years underground,

101
00:07:06,941 --> 00:07:09,820
most need a good cleaning to rid them of contaminants,

102
00:07:09,821 --> 00:07:12,940
especially sulfur.

103
00:07:12,941 --> 00:07:16,280
In this process, we bring the oil in and mix it with hydrogen.

104
00:07:16,281 --> 00:07:18,940
We pass it through the heat exchangers behind me

105
00:07:18,941 --> 00:07:21,800
and the heater to my left, and we heat the oil up

106
00:07:21,801 --> 00:07:23,080
to about 700 degrees.

107
00:07:23,081 --> 00:07:25,380
At that point, we pass it over a catalyst,

108
00:07:25,381 --> 00:07:27,440
and that catalyst, along with the hydrogen,

109
00:07:27,441 --> 00:07:30,060
removes contaminants in the oil before we send it on

110
00:07:30,061 --> 00:07:32,120
for further processing.

111
00:07:32,121 --> 00:07:33,120
The result?

112
00:07:33,121 --> 00:07:37,580
A clean, contaminant-free product, like this jet fuel.

113
00:07:37,581 --> 00:07:40,240
In fact, most airports maintain direct pipelines

114
00:07:40,241 --> 00:07:41,300
to local refineries.

115
00:07:48,240 --> 00:07:50,480
And as for all that removed sulfur,

116
00:07:50,481 --> 00:07:53,620
it's collected and sold as a key ingredient

117
00:07:53,621 --> 00:07:58,100
in agricultural fertilizers, in rubber tire manufacture,

118
00:07:58,101 --> 00:08:02,800
in fire extinguishers, and even in explosives.

119
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:07,480
But not every fraction is ready yet.

120
00:08:08,680 --> 00:08:11,480
And some even need an atomic makeover.

121
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:18,760
Cracking shatters larger, complex hydrocarbon molecules

122
00:08:18,761 --> 00:08:20,680
into simpler, more useful ones,

123
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,220
which are then made into finished products.

124
00:08:24,700 --> 00:08:27,560
This can be done either directly with intense heat,

125
00:08:28,100 --> 00:08:31,100
or in combination with chemical catalysts.

126
00:08:31,101 --> 00:08:34,100
You can see this is the sort of catalyst we use.

127
00:08:34,101 --> 00:08:36,100
It almost moves like a liquid.

128
00:08:36,101 --> 00:08:40,100
What we do is we mix this catalyst at very high temperature

129
00:08:40,101 --> 00:08:43,100
with the gas oil, over 1300 degrees,

130
00:08:43,101 --> 00:08:47,100
and it reacts to form these lighter products that you see here.

131
00:08:47,101 --> 00:08:51,100
Cracking heavy gas oils into smaller hydrocarbon molecules

132
00:08:51,101 --> 00:08:56,100
creates this base oil used to power ships.

133
00:08:56,101 --> 00:09:00,380
In addition, the cracking makes propylene, captured in this canister.

134
00:09:01,380 --> 00:09:10,560
Propylene is a feedstock used in makeup, toothpaste, antifreeze, paints, and polyurethane products, including football helmets.

135
00:09:11,080 --> 00:09:11,780
Go.

136
00:09:15,960 --> 00:09:20,440
No new oil refinery has been built in the United States since 1974.

137
00:09:20,441 --> 00:09:24,440
only through new efficiencies have refiners been able to keep

138
00:09:24,540 --> 00:09:27,740
up with ever rising demand.

139
00:09:27,840 --> 00:09:29,780
We don't throw anything away.

140
00:09:29,880 --> 00:09:33,820
The barrel of crude is much too valuable a resource not to try

141
00:09:33,920 --> 00:09:35,760
to use everything we can out of it.

142
00:09:35,860 --> 00:09:39,320
And we try to use 100% of that barrel.

143
00:09:39,420 --> 00:09:41,400
Throughout its history, the industry

144
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:44,900
has used chance discoveries to turn what seemed like waste

145
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,500
into useful products.

146
00:09:47,501 --> 00:09:54,500
Rod wax was a whitish goo that came out of the ground on drill bits and often clogged the mechanisms.

147
00:09:54,501 --> 00:10:03,500
Only after learning that oil workers used the rod wax as a balm for cuts did chemist Robert Chesbrough begin to toy with it,

148
00:10:03,501 --> 00:10:08,500
later naming his new product Vaseline.

149
00:10:08,501 --> 00:10:12,500
Although no longer scraped from rigs, today a jar of Vaseline

150
00:10:12,600 --> 00:10:15,640
is sold every 39 seconds as a byproduct

151
00:10:15,740 --> 00:10:17,240
of refined lubricant oils.

152
00:10:21,640 --> 00:10:25,620
Petroleum coke, the nasty black residue left after all the other

153
00:10:25,720 --> 00:10:29,820
fractions are removed, was deemed the bottom of the barrel

154
00:10:29,920 --> 00:10:34,060
until used in ironworks to help fuel their furnaces.

155
00:10:34,160 --> 00:10:35,720
Waste is a resource.

156
00:10:35,721 --> 00:10:39,720
So they started looking for and finding uses for these various

157
00:10:39,721 --> 00:10:42,720
byproducts that were created in the refining process.

158
00:10:42,721 --> 00:10:47,720
And in 1910, a Ford Model T owner urged a scientist named

159
00:10:47,721 --> 00:10:50,720
Walter Snelling to examine vapors rising from his gas tank

160
00:10:50,721 --> 00:10:52,720
vents.

161
00:10:52,721 --> 00:10:57,720
Two years later, Snelling filed a patent for the oil-borne gas,

162
00:10:57,721 --> 00:10:59,720
propane.

163
00:10:59,721 --> 00:11:03,720
Propane is one of those fuels that nobody makes on purpose.

164
00:11:03,721 --> 00:11:17,553
It is a byproduct, and we've been able to basically as an industry build a business on a product that in the early years of the industry was literally flared off as a

165
00:11:17,554 --> 00:11:18,720
waste product.

166
00:11:18,721 --> 00:11:25,720
Propane arises at multiple stages during refining. Think of it as pure bonus.

167
00:11:25,721 --> 00:11:28,160
It's an energy-rich hydrocarbon.

168
00:11:28,260 --> 00:11:31,960
It has about 2 and 3 quarter times as much hydrogen per

169
00:11:32,060 --> 00:11:34,460
molecule as, say, a natural gas molecule.

170
00:11:34,560 --> 00:11:37,160
It's also a very, very clean burning fuel,

171
00:11:37,260 --> 00:11:40,560
and it's cost-effective.

172
00:11:40,660 --> 00:11:47,880
Propane produces 18 to 24% less CO2 than gasoline.

173
00:11:47,980 --> 00:11:50,820
Today, 17 million homes rely on propane

174
00:11:50,920 --> 00:11:53,120
for home heating, cooking, and power.

175
00:11:55,720 --> 00:12:00,720
Organic farmers use its heat to wither weeds in their fields

176
00:12:00,820 --> 00:12:03,820
and to sanitize livestock houses without chemicals.

177
00:12:07,960 --> 00:12:10,260
And of course, more than 50 million homes

178
00:12:10,360 --> 00:12:13,980
use it to fire up the family barbecue.

179
00:12:14,080 --> 00:12:17,980
Imagine 2% of the energy market being tapped in

180
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:21,480
by 50 million plus folks just for barbecuing.

181
00:12:21,481 --> 00:12:22,362
barbecuing.

182
00:12:22,363 --> 00:12:28,540
Portability has always been a prime advantage of all oil and its derivatives.

183
00:12:29,940 --> 00:12:35,920
Compared to coal, for example, it's an easy resource to move. Oil is very versatile,

184
00:12:36,440 --> 00:12:41,780
and one of the key things about it is that it's a liquid, which makes it easily transportable.

185
00:12:42,940 --> 00:12:44,650
Propane is no exception.

186
00:12:44,651 --> 00:12:49,780
Manufacturers put the gas under pressure, which causes it to reform as a

187
00:12:49,781 --> 00:12:56,780
liquid, making it 270 times more compact.

188
00:12:56,781 --> 00:13:00,780
Because it can be stored as a liquid and consumed as a gas,

189
00:13:00,781 --> 00:13:03,780
you have a great deal of options in terms of the type of

190
00:13:03,781 --> 00:13:06,780
applications that it can be put to, and that seems sort of

191
00:13:06,781 --> 00:13:09,780
counterintuitive in many respects, but that's one of the

192
00:13:09,781 --> 00:13:12,780
unique properties of propane is that it can be a liquid and it

193
00:13:12,781 --> 00:13:15,780
can be used as a gas as well.

194
00:13:15,781 --> 00:13:18,780
Only when the tank valve is opened and the pressure

195
00:13:18,781 --> 00:13:21,780
the pressure inside normalized as the liquid propane

196
00:13:21,781 --> 00:13:24,780
returned to its original gas form.

197
00:13:24,781 --> 00:13:27,780
And at that point, it's ready to roll.

198
00:13:27,781 --> 00:13:30,780
Propane is the most widely used alternative motor fuel

199
00:13:30,781 --> 00:13:31,780
in the world.

200
00:13:31,781 --> 00:13:36,780
There are about 11 million vehicles that operate on propane every day.

201
00:13:36,781 --> 00:13:39,780
Here in the United States, anyone that's been to Las Vegas

202
00:13:39,781 --> 00:13:42,780
and taken a taxi cab from the airport to a hotel

203
00:13:42,781 --> 00:13:46,780
is ridden in a propane-powered taxi cab.

204
00:13:46,781 --> 00:13:49,780
But if you think that's all there is to oil, answer this.

205
00:13:50,720 --> 00:13:55,180
What do hot tubs, packing peanuts, and top-secret weapons depots have in common?

206
00:13:56,600 --> 00:13:58,380
One word, plastics.

207
00:13:59,140 --> 00:14:00,940
Petroleum's most prolific byproduct.

208
00:14:03,540 --> 00:14:08,660
In the early 1900s, a young entrepreneur observed his sister, Mabel,

209
00:14:09,180 --> 00:14:13,200
mix coal dust with oil-based Vaseline and apply it to her eyelashes.

210
00:14:14,200 --> 00:14:16,320
Soon after, he created Mabelene.

211
00:14:16,780 --> 00:14:19,240
one of America's leading cosmetics brands.

212
00:14:20,320 --> 00:14:25,180
Secrets of Oil will return on Modern Marvels, here on History.

213
00:14:28,700 --> 00:14:33,620
Each year, American refineries process more than 6 billion barrels of oil.

214
00:14:35,080 --> 00:14:36,760
But here's a stunning fact.

215
00:14:37,220 --> 00:14:41,500
From less than 5% of that comes more than 100 billion pounds of plastic.

216
00:14:41,501 --> 00:14:48,500
That's more plastic than the combined weight of every man, woman and child in the United States.

217
00:14:48,501 --> 00:14:51,500
Plastic is oil's most abundant finished product.

218
00:14:51,501 --> 00:14:55,500
We can't live without it, and it's getting expensive.

219
00:14:55,501 --> 00:15:00,500
Even something as simple as a paper cup, its functionality is based on having plastic on the inside here.

220
00:15:00,501 --> 00:15:04,500
Because if you didn't have that plastic, your latte would be all over your lap

221
00:15:04,501 --> 00:15:10,500
because it's the plastic that seals the liquids inside and allows this cup to do its job.

222
00:15:11,500 --> 00:15:14,140
Monomers, the chemicals from which plastics are made,

223
00:15:14,640 --> 00:15:18,200
come from the gases collected during refining of oil and natural gas.

224
00:15:19,780 --> 00:15:24,280
Like other fractions, these gases are cracked into smaller hydrocarbon elements,

225
00:15:24,540 --> 00:15:26,220
known as chemical feedstocks.

226
00:15:27,320 --> 00:15:32,220
From seven basic feedstocks, including ethylene, propylene, and benzene,

227
00:15:32,780 --> 00:15:35,120
nearly all the plastic in the world is manufactured.

228
00:15:36,620 --> 00:15:39,300
Dylon is an example where we can actually show you how it's made.

229
00:15:39,301 --> 00:15:44,320
So we add in here a diacid, and then we're going to add a diamine.

230
00:15:45,640 --> 00:15:52,620
And at the interface of these two chemicals, both are derived from oil, you end up with nylon.

231
00:15:56,100 --> 00:16:02,300
Each year, more than 8 billion pounds of nylon make everything from stockings to parachutes.

232
00:16:05,760 --> 00:16:08,500
But nylon's not alone when it comes to many uses.

233
00:16:09,300 --> 00:16:14,300
Nearly every plastic can be formed and shaped in infinite ways.

234
00:16:14,301 --> 00:16:17,300
That's what makes it so valuable.

235
00:16:17,301 --> 00:16:22,300
A lot of folks think that plastics have always been cheap, you know, but that isn't true.

236
00:16:22,301 --> 00:16:26,300
Actually, for a long time, plastics have been more expensive than metals.

237
00:16:26,301 --> 00:16:33,300
The part of it that's cheap is manufacturing all of those different types of things.

238
00:16:33,301 --> 00:16:36,300
Spartak Plastics is an international manufacturer

239
00:16:36,301 --> 00:16:38,300
of plastic materials.

240
00:16:41,300 --> 00:16:45,300
With an annual production of more than 1.7 billion pounds,

241
00:16:48,300 --> 00:16:51,300
their materials can be found in everything from food packaging

242
00:16:51,301 --> 00:16:55,300
to tanning beds to bullet-resistant windows.

243
00:17:03,300 --> 00:17:06,200
Here at their La Mirada, California facility,

244
00:17:06,300 --> 00:17:09,140
the focus is on plastic sheeting and rolls,

245
00:17:09,240 --> 00:17:13,240
made from two of the more prominent plastics.

246
00:17:13,340 --> 00:17:17,940
This plant primarily runs ABS or polystyrene.

247
00:17:18,040 --> 00:17:21,660
They're both a styrenic-based material.

248
00:17:21,760 --> 00:17:24,160
Every time you open your refrigerator door, the shelf,

249
00:17:24,260 --> 00:17:26,560
the food liners, and certainly the door,

250
00:17:26,660 --> 00:17:29,500
that's polystyrene as well.

251
00:17:29,600 --> 00:17:32,800
ABS and polystyrene arrive at the Spartek facility

252
00:17:32,801 --> 00:17:36,600
in the form of plastic pellets.

253
00:17:36,700 --> 00:17:41,300
Some are virgin stock, others recycled.

254
00:17:41,400 --> 00:17:43,640
Air hoses move them throughout the facility

255
00:17:43,740 --> 00:17:46,440
until they reach the extruder, the heart of the operation.

256
00:17:49,740 --> 00:17:52,920
Inside this heat extruder is a long auger screw that

257
00:17:53,020 --> 00:17:55,680
steadily rotates, pulling the plastic pellets

258
00:17:55,780 --> 00:17:56,860
toward its opposite end.

259
00:17:59,320 --> 00:18:02,800
As the pellets compress and rub against one another,

260
00:18:02,801 --> 00:18:06,340
friction and heat are created, causing the plastic to melt.

261
00:18:07,940 --> 00:18:10,900
The taffy-like mix is then pushed through a thin die,

262
00:18:11,740 --> 00:18:14,940
forming a plastic sheet that threads across a series of rollers.

263
00:18:15,840 --> 00:18:20,280
It may look fairly simple, but there's a lot of geometry

264
00:18:20,281 --> 00:18:23,420
and a lot of calculations that go into the design of a screw,

265
00:18:23,640 --> 00:18:27,000
and a lot of that has just been derived by trial and error over time.

266
00:18:29,700 --> 00:18:32,420
This roll stock will be used in various packaging,

267
00:18:32,800 --> 00:18:38,440
while a quick change to a larger die results in plastic sheets

268
00:18:38,441 --> 00:18:46,200
that can be shaped into single plastic pieces for spas, boats, even kayaks.

269
00:18:47,840 --> 00:18:52,060
You put this sheet in a couple of ovens and you just gently heat it on there,

270
00:18:52,200 --> 00:18:55,080
as opposed to the really vigorous heating they went through to melt it.

271
00:18:55,380 --> 00:18:58,600
It will begin to soften until it begins to just sag,

272
00:18:58,660 --> 00:19:01,320
and that's what they'll refer to it as the sag that it's got.

273
00:19:01,321 --> 00:19:11,065
That's moved around then and put over a mold, and the old vacuum forming technique just pulls it down and sucks it down to make the form of whatever part it is that

274
00:19:11,066 --> 00:19:11,766
you want.

275
00:19:12,840 --> 00:19:16,000
Thermoforming is a key method for custom plastic manufacturing.

276
00:19:18,740 --> 00:19:22,340
But there are others, including welding.

277
00:19:24,500 --> 00:19:30,160
Vinyl, also known as PVC, is a petro-based plastic known for its durability and flexibility.

278
00:19:31,320 --> 00:19:41,000
Its practicalities were first teed up in the 1920s by a chemical engineer in search of, legend holds, a better golf ball.

279
00:19:43,360 --> 00:19:47,200
Today, oil-based plastics still play a role in golf ball manufacture.

280
00:19:53,840 --> 00:20:00,760
And PVC's advantages have spread across industry, health care, and the military.

281
00:20:01,320 --> 00:20:02,893
Since 1997, the U.S.

282
00:20:02,894 --> 00:20:08,320
military has destroyed nearly 55% of its Cold War arsenal of chemical

283
00:20:08,321 --> 00:20:09,320
weapons.

284
00:20:09,321 --> 00:20:17,320
But it's still got nearly 14,000 tons to go, and surrounded by an environment filled with

285
00:20:17,321 --> 00:20:20,320
deadly agents like sarin.

286
00:20:20,321 --> 00:20:26,320
A worker's protective suit means the difference between life and death.

287
00:20:26,321 --> 00:20:29,720
Here at Vinyl Technology, they've manufactured more than

288
00:20:29,820 --> 00:20:33,660
130,000 of these life-saving vinyl suits.

289
00:20:33,760 --> 00:20:37,960
The suit is basically 99% plastic material.

290
00:20:38,060 --> 00:20:41,760
The visor is basically convertible window material,

291
00:20:41,860 --> 00:20:43,880
which you'll find in convertible cars.

292
00:20:43,980 --> 00:20:48,080
Everything else is a special alloy of plastic films that's

293
00:20:48,180 --> 00:20:51,980
been specially developed for the chemical agent protection.

294
00:20:52,080 --> 00:20:54,820
It's made to slow down the permeation of that agent

295
00:20:54,821 --> 00:20:59,500
through the material to provide the worker with as long a working time as possible.

296
00:20:59,501 --> 00:21:05,163
There are no sewn parts or stitching on the suits.

297
00:21:05,164 --> 00:21:09,580
Instead, all the seams are welded using

298
00:21:09,581 --> 00:21:12,820
ultra-high radio frequencies to fuse the materials together.

299
00:21:12,821 --> 00:21:19,000
If you take a look, you'll see some of the intricate welds that go around the perimeter

300
00:21:19,001 --> 00:21:23,200
of the visor, welding the vinyl to the alloy,

301
00:21:23,201 --> 00:21:24,740
which is the white material.

302
00:21:24,741 --> 00:21:26,440
All the seams are extremely clean,

303
00:21:26,441 --> 00:21:27,800
and the seams are on the outside

304
00:21:27,801 --> 00:21:31,580
and provide the maximum comfort for the wearer.

305
00:21:31,581 --> 00:21:34,040
The suits aren't completed until they meet the workers

306
00:21:34,041 --> 00:21:35,360
who wear them.

307
00:21:35,361 --> 00:21:37,360
When the workers are preparing for an entry,

308
00:21:37,361 --> 00:21:40,360
they will don the suit, and then they will physically

309
00:21:40,361 --> 00:21:43,900
get heat sealed into the suit using a similar type of machine

310
00:21:43,901 --> 00:21:45,760
that we've used to manufacture the suits.

311
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:52,600
So once they're in, they're in for good.

312
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:56,160
They do their work, and they physically get cut out of the suit,

313
00:21:56,340 --> 00:21:58,080
and the suit is disposed of at that point.

314
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:02,260
So oil is effective at keeping deadly chemicals out.

315
00:22:03,800 --> 00:22:06,480
But what happens if you swallow it by mistake?

316
00:22:08,080 --> 00:22:10,780
Odds are, you already have.

317
00:22:13,520 --> 00:22:17,400
Roughly 9% of every oil barrel's output is jet fuel,

318
00:22:17,401 --> 00:22:20,800
and the airline industry burns through 19 billion gallons

319
00:22:20,900 --> 00:22:22,300
of it annually.

320
00:22:22,400 --> 00:22:24,700
Add just one penny to its cost per gallon,

321
00:22:24,800 --> 00:22:29,540
and airline expenses soar more than $180 million.

322
00:22:29,640 --> 00:22:33,040
Secrets of Oil will return on Modern Marvels,

323
00:22:33,140 --> 00:22:36,260
here on History.

324
00:22:36,360 --> 00:22:38,860
When it comes to big, there isn't much that's

325
00:22:38,960 --> 00:22:41,760
larger than the crawler transporter,

326
00:22:41,860 --> 00:22:44,200
the vehicle that moves the space shuttle from point A

327
00:22:44,300 --> 00:22:47,400
to point B.

328
00:22:47,401 --> 00:22:53,380
So it's hard to imagine that this 6 million pound behemoth has much in common with an ordinary watch.

329
00:22:54,700 --> 00:23:00,580
Despite their size difference, beneath their exteriors, both mechanisms have lots of moving parts.

330
00:23:01,700 --> 00:23:08,580
And whether they weigh a few tons or a few microns, moving parts almost always need oil-based lubricants.

331
00:23:10,340 --> 00:23:14,920
A lubricant is designed to really do two different things.

332
00:23:14,921 --> 00:23:20,760
One is to prevent wear, and the other is whenever you have moving parts, there's heat generated.

333
00:23:21,360 --> 00:23:28,240
So it really lubricates by taking the heat away that's formed and also to keep the surfaces from rubbing together.

334
00:23:30,260 --> 00:23:34,820
Lubricants are made from base oils fractioned from the heavier elements off the distillation tower.

335
00:23:36,960 --> 00:23:44,120
They're considered among the most valuable fractions, even as they represent less than 8% of a barrel's output.

336
00:23:44,920 --> 00:23:48,680
That's because they do so many things,

337
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:51,760
from helping to move the shuttle to steering a missile

338
00:23:51,761 --> 00:23:57,500
to ensuring the chain on the chainsaw, saws.

339
00:24:02,780 --> 00:24:04,780
Manufacturer Lubricating Specialties

340
00:24:04,781 --> 00:24:08,020
receives base oils from refineries all over the country

341
00:24:08,021 --> 00:24:12,000
and blends them into more than a thousand different lubes

342
00:24:12,001 --> 00:24:12,701
every month.

343
00:24:14,920 --> 00:24:21,520
Of course, we make motor oils.

344
00:24:21,521 --> 00:24:24,640
That's probably our number one product that we make.

345
00:24:24,641 --> 00:24:28,880
We do specialty things with fire-resistant hydraulic fluids, which are very important

346
00:24:28,881 --> 00:24:32,700
in foundries, so if you get a leak, it doesn't catch fire.

347
00:24:32,701 --> 00:24:38,640
We also manufacture Navy oils that are used in the hydraulics on ships to move the rudders,

348
00:24:38,641 --> 00:24:42,680
missile batteries, or guns.

349
00:24:42,681 --> 00:24:45,420
and also the catapult oil to launch the airplanes.

350
00:24:52,260 --> 00:24:55,120
Any lubricant's job is to form a thin film

351
00:24:55,121 --> 00:24:56,620
between two moving parts.

352
00:24:57,500 --> 00:24:59,520
It must be thick enough to withstand the friction

353
00:24:59,521 --> 00:25:00,960
generated as the parts work.

354
00:25:03,080 --> 00:25:05,500
Yet not so thick as to inhibit or degrade

355
00:25:05,501 --> 00:25:06,460
the part's performance.

356
00:25:09,380 --> 00:25:11,960
Viscosity is the term used to describe that thickness.

357
00:25:12,680 --> 00:25:15,880
and it's determined by the blend of base oils from the refinery

358
00:25:15,881 --> 00:25:18,680
with additional chemical additives.

359
00:25:18,681 --> 00:25:23,820
This is what we call the blending area, or fondly known as the snake pit.

360
00:25:23,821 --> 00:25:27,160
This is where we take and blend all the raw materials.

361
00:25:27,161 --> 00:25:29,160
Some are easy, one or two ingredients,

362
00:25:29,161 --> 00:25:32,600
some are complicated and have up to 20 ingredients.

363
00:25:32,601 --> 00:25:34,740
Some are liquid, some are solids.

364
00:25:34,741 --> 00:25:37,880
So this is where you make your money or you lose your money.

365
00:25:37,881 --> 00:25:41,540
Because if it goes wrong here, it's expensive.

366
00:25:41,541 --> 00:25:44,580
One of those additives is itself made from oil,

367
00:25:44,581 --> 00:25:50,480
and it puts the W, or winter, into 5W30.

368
00:25:50,481 --> 00:25:53,220
It's called a viscosity index improver

369
00:25:53,221 --> 00:25:56,080
and comes in 50-pound blocks.

370
00:25:56,081 --> 00:25:57,360
But check this out.

371
00:25:57,361 --> 00:25:59,500
It's not a solid at all.

372
00:25:59,501 --> 00:26:01,060
It's something that's cold flowed.

373
00:26:01,061 --> 00:26:03,720
So as we sit here, we open the side here.

374
00:26:03,721 --> 00:26:05,160
If we came back tomorrow morning,

375
00:26:05,161 --> 00:26:06,560
it would all be on the ground.

376
00:26:06,561 --> 00:26:08,500
Even though it appears as a solid,

377
00:26:08,501 --> 00:26:12,280
it's really still a liquid, a very viscous, thick liquid.

378
00:26:13,180 --> 00:26:16,680
The improver is chopped, heated, and then blended into the motor oil

379
00:26:16,681 --> 00:26:20,720
to ensure that the oil itself continues to flow in all seasons.

380
00:26:23,140 --> 00:26:28,020
Because this is the substance that allows us to use the same motor oil in the winter as the summer.

381
00:26:28,021 --> 00:26:30,560
This expands very, very rapidly.

382
00:26:31,280 --> 00:26:35,240
In the cold state, it's more like a watch spring, very tightly wound together.

383
00:26:35,241 --> 00:26:37,240
But when it gets hot at 200 degrees,

384
00:26:37,340 --> 00:26:39,880
it really expands almost like a corkscrew.

385
00:26:39,980 --> 00:26:43,240
It gets very, very long and acts like a bunch of fibers

386
00:26:43,340 --> 00:26:46,680
to keep the thin motor oil flowing very quickly.

387
00:26:46,780 --> 00:26:48,580
It makes it seem thicker.

388
00:26:48,680 --> 00:26:50,820
So we can get a good cold temperature

389
00:26:50,920 --> 00:26:54,220
and a good high temperature.

390
00:26:54,320 --> 00:26:57,360
Today, few processes are as automated as food production

391
00:26:57,460 --> 00:26:59,100
and packaging.

392
00:26:59,200 --> 00:27:02,260
And here, too, the machines need a specialized lube called

393
00:27:02,360 --> 00:27:03,900
food grade oil.

394
00:27:03,901 --> 00:27:05,900
It's even okay to swallow.

395
00:27:05,901 --> 00:27:12,900
These are required by the FDA to be used so that if accidentally the oil would get into

396
00:27:12,901 --> 00:27:15,900
some of the foods, it's no harm to you.

397
00:27:15,901 --> 00:27:16,900
This is hydrocarbon.

398
00:27:16,901 --> 00:27:18,900
That's been extra processed, extra clean.

399
00:27:18,901 --> 00:27:21,900
It's handled food grade all the way.

400
00:27:21,901 --> 00:27:23,900
Special packaging, special drums.

401
00:27:23,901 --> 00:27:26,900
So it never comes in contact with any metal.

402
00:27:26,901 --> 00:27:31,900
But if the prospect that you might be eating oil surprises you, don't forget, you're also

403
00:27:31,901 --> 00:27:33,900
you're also rubbing it all over.

404
00:27:33,901 --> 00:27:36,900
The baby oil that you buy is 100% this,

405
00:27:36,901 --> 00:27:38,900
with a little fragrance.

406
00:27:38,901 --> 00:27:41,900
Suntan lotion is probably 80% this

407
00:27:41,901 --> 00:27:43,900
with a little bit of emulsifier.

408
00:27:43,901 --> 00:27:47,900
Lipstick is about 70% this with a lot of dyes

409
00:27:47,901 --> 00:27:51,900
and also some wax products to give it some consistency.

410
00:27:51,901 --> 00:27:55,900
It's used in just about everything that we put on ourselves.

411
00:27:55,901 --> 00:27:58,900
For most lubricants, it's one and done.

412
00:27:58,901 --> 00:28:03,140
When done, use it, replace it, and move on.

413
00:28:03,141 --> 00:28:06,740
But with oil prices on the rise, the world's most common lube,

414
00:28:06,741 --> 00:28:10,440
motor oil, can have a second life.

415
00:28:10,441 --> 00:28:13,740
There's 1.4 billion gallons of used oil generated in the

416
00:28:13,741 --> 00:28:15,520
United States every year.

417
00:28:15,521 --> 00:28:18,960
Here at Evergreen Oil, we take that used oil and we re-refine

418
00:28:18,961 --> 00:28:23,000
it, meaning we take the used oil and take it back to a near

419
00:28:23,001 --> 00:28:24,160
virgin state.

420
00:28:24,161 --> 00:28:28,560
We calculate we can use that same gallon over 20 times.

421
00:28:28,561 --> 00:28:31,120
Re-refining is different from reconditioned motor oil,

422
00:28:31,500 --> 00:28:33,300
which is often labeled second use,

423
00:28:33,720 --> 00:28:37,140
and is simply filtered, with sometimes inconsistent results.

424
00:28:39,160 --> 00:28:41,740
Evergreen Oil estimates that full-scale re-refining

425
00:28:41,741 --> 00:28:45,000
could save upwards of 90,000 barrels of oil a day.

426
00:28:46,400 --> 00:28:48,500
So here's the used oil we just collected

427
00:28:48,501 --> 00:28:49,820
and brought here to Evergreen Oil.

428
00:28:50,260 --> 00:28:53,860
It's full of contaminants such as heavy metals, sulfur.

429
00:28:54,340 --> 00:28:56,660
It might have some concentrations of halogens.

430
00:28:57,180 --> 00:28:57,920
It's dirty.

431
00:28:57,921 --> 00:29:00,580
we're going to take that and put it through our re-refinery

432
00:29:00,581 --> 00:29:03,020
and process it into a reusable base loop.

433
00:29:04,060 --> 00:29:07,400
Re-refining works nearly backwards from the original refining process.

434
00:29:08,540 --> 00:29:12,320
First, the used oil is hydro-treated to remove not just the pollutants,

435
00:29:12,460 --> 00:29:13,540
but the additives as well.

436
00:29:14,620 --> 00:29:16,560
Remember that viscosity index improver?

437
00:29:17,260 --> 00:29:19,220
Well, in re-refining, it's got to go.

438
00:29:20,300 --> 00:29:23,900
What's left is then re-distilled, creating a near-pure fraction.

439
00:29:23,901 --> 00:29:28,900
This is our re-refined base lube.

440
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:30,900
End of the process.

441
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:33,900
All of the metals, all of the contaminants,

442
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:36,840
the additive package has been removed, all the water,

443
00:29:36,940 --> 00:29:38,740
all the light ends.

444
00:29:38,840 --> 00:29:41,760
And we've taken it to a near virgin state.

445
00:29:41,860 --> 00:29:45,660
And this is good as gold right here.

446
00:29:45,760 --> 00:29:49,800
Today, Evergreen re-refines more than 14 million gallons

447
00:29:49,900 --> 00:29:51,800
of used motor oil a year.

448
00:29:51,801 --> 00:29:58,800
The energy required to re-refine motor oil is close to 50% less than it takes to refine fresh crude.

449
00:29:58,801 --> 00:30:04,800
No doubt that's not just good business, but good for the environment as well.

450
00:30:04,801 --> 00:30:10,800
But motor oil is not the only recycled product when the time comes for the rubber to meet the road.

451
00:30:10,801 --> 00:30:13,800
And this one really rocks.

452
00:30:13,801 --> 00:30:19,720
really rocks.

453
00:30:19,721 --> 00:30:23,180
Paraffin wax is a byproduct of lubricant oil refining.

454
00:30:23,181 --> 00:30:29,240
Not only is it non-toxic, each year it's shaped into more than 3 billion Crayola crayons,

455
00:30:29,241 --> 00:30:34,500
1.5 billion paintballs, and 1 billion pounds of candle wax.

456
00:30:34,501 --> 00:30:40,640
Secrets of Oil will return on Modern Marvels.

457
00:30:40,641 --> 00:30:44,980
Scorching sun, raging storms, vicious winds.

458
00:30:46,680 --> 00:30:51,540
No matter the conditions, we depend on our homes to withstand Mother Nature's fury.

459
00:30:52,680 --> 00:30:56,140
And nowhere does that protection matter more than on the roof.

460
00:30:57,180 --> 00:30:58,020
Enter oil.

461
00:30:59,540 --> 00:31:03,100
Today, four out of five U.S. homes are topped with asphalt shingles,

462
00:31:03,101 --> 00:31:05,500
the most common roofing material in the country.

463
00:31:05,501 --> 00:31:14,520
The asphalt industry manufactures 12.5 billion square feet of roofing shingle product on an annual basis.

464
00:31:14,521 --> 00:31:17,860
That's enough material to cover 5 million homes per year.

465
00:31:20,580 --> 00:31:24,260
Natural deposits of asphalt have existed for millions of years.

466
00:31:27,280 --> 00:31:35,020
And the ancients relied upon its sticky properties to build cities, waterproof boats, and even embalm mummies.

467
00:31:35,500 --> 00:31:43,940
Today, nearly all asphalt is derived from oil refining and makes up 3% of each barrel

468
00:31:43,941 --> 00:31:46,780
of crude.

469
00:31:46,781 --> 00:31:54,800
It's the dense, dark liquid that remains after the other, lighter hydrocarbons are removed.

470
00:31:54,801 --> 00:31:59,680
Here at the Owens Corning Asphalt Shingle Manufacturing Center in Compton, California,

471
00:31:59,681 --> 00:32:03,900
they convert more than 110 tons of liquid asphalt every day.

472
00:32:03,901 --> 00:32:07,580
This is where the asphalt comes into our facility.

473
00:32:07,740 --> 00:32:12,340
It arrives in trucks and then is transferred into the converter,

474
00:32:12,480 --> 00:32:14,940
which is the tower that's standing directly behind me.

475
00:32:15,320 --> 00:32:20,360
This is where we actually convert the asphalt into a roofing grade material.

476
00:32:21,080 --> 00:32:24,860
Hot air is blown into the asphalt to make it both soft and durable.

477
00:32:26,560 --> 00:32:29,300
Asphalt shingles are made from three primary elements.

478
00:32:30,100 --> 00:32:32,620
A synthetic mat made from paper or fiberglass.

479
00:32:32,621 --> 00:32:38,620
mineral granules, and finally the newly blown asphalt.

480
00:33:02,620 --> 00:33:06,900
Through significant advances in technology of the fiberglass mat,

481
00:33:07,160 --> 00:33:10,660
we've also been able to reduce the amount of asphalt required

482
00:33:10,661 --> 00:33:14,420
while still ensuring a high-performing shilo on your roof.

483
00:33:16,680 --> 00:33:19,080
Granules on the top of the shingle are coarse

484
00:33:19,081 --> 00:33:20,520
and coated with different colors.

485
00:33:22,540 --> 00:33:24,860
This offers buyers aesthetic choices,

486
00:33:25,380 --> 00:33:29,400
but also has the practical impact of deflecting the sun's UV rays,

487
00:33:29,800 --> 00:33:30,540
reducing heat.

488
00:33:32,620 --> 00:33:36,620
Once the materials are fused, the shingles are cooled,

489
00:33:36,621 --> 00:33:40,620
then cut and finished in many ways

490
00:33:40,621 --> 00:33:42,620
to meet different markets.

491
00:33:42,621 --> 00:33:45,620
Here we are at the end of the line.

492
00:33:45,621 --> 00:33:47,620
This is the finished shingled product.

493
00:33:47,621 --> 00:33:50,620
You can see that now we have the asphalt,

494
00:33:50,621 --> 00:33:53,620
the mat inside, the granules impregnated.

