00:33:53,621 --> 00:33:56,620
It's all come together in the finished product.

496
00:33:56,621 --> 00:34:00,620
On the flip side, you can see the backdusting material,

497
00:34:00,621 --> 00:34:05,540
material, the adhesive where it's going to seal one shingle to the other on the rooftop,

498
00:34:06,220 --> 00:34:10,080
and a strip of material so that they don't stick together when they're in the package

499
00:34:10,081 --> 00:34:11,860
in the bundle before you get them on the roof.

500
00:34:14,160 --> 00:34:18,820
Today, roofing accounts for less than 15% of all asphalt that leaves the refinery.

501
00:34:20,700 --> 00:34:26,140
A still smaller amount goes into building water-resistant structures like seawalls, piers, and dikes.

502
00:34:27,320 --> 00:34:28,600
And where does the rest go?

503
00:34:28,601 --> 00:34:32,600
Look no further than the road beneath your feet.

504
00:34:32,700 --> 00:34:36,200
There are 4 million miles of roads in the United States,

505
00:34:36,300 --> 00:34:40,640
94% of which are covered in asphalt.

506
00:34:40,740 --> 00:34:43,640
Vulcan asphalt produces upwards of 500 tons

507
00:34:43,740 --> 00:34:45,620
of paving asphalt an hour.

508
00:34:48,180 --> 00:34:50,960
And none of it would hold together without oil.

509
00:34:51,060 --> 00:34:54,820
We have to have that oil to use as a binder,

510
00:34:54,920 --> 00:34:56,920
which binds it and holds it together.

511
00:34:56,921 --> 00:34:59,160
together that gives us our compaction

512
00:34:59,260 --> 00:35:02,420
and helps make everything solid that we could drive on.

513
00:35:02,520 --> 00:35:04,620
So it's a very, very important ingredient.

514
00:35:10,360 --> 00:35:13,500
Paving asphalt is made from sand

515
00:35:13,600 --> 00:35:17,040
and various sized crushed rock known as aggregate,

516
00:35:17,140 --> 00:35:18,940
which are mixed together into a blend.

517
00:35:22,040 --> 00:35:24,680
As you can see, I have various different sizes,

518
00:35:24,681 --> 00:35:28,680
some 3 quarters, some half-inch, 3 eighths, sand.

519
00:35:28,681 --> 00:35:32,680
These are the various sizes that we use in the mixture.

520
00:35:32,681 --> 00:35:36,680
In order to make a good blend, you want to fill up every

521
00:35:36,681 --> 00:35:40,680
void. You don't want any cracks. So what you do is you start out with your top

522
00:35:40,681 --> 00:35:44,680
size and you put in smaller sizes so that it all

523
00:35:44,681 --> 00:35:48,680
compacts in together. Otherwise the road will tear apart on you.

524
00:35:48,681 --> 00:35:52,680
The oil fills in any cracks and it also binds it together.

525
00:35:54,680 --> 00:35:58,380
Different surface types require different mixtures.

526
00:35:58,480 --> 00:36:01,420
Once the right formula is set, the aggregate blend

527
00:36:01,520 --> 00:36:03,720
is conveyed into a dryer, where it's

528
00:36:03,820 --> 00:36:07,820
heated to nearly 1,600 degrees Fahrenheit.

529
00:36:07,920 --> 00:36:11,260
We want it completely dry before we mix the oil

530
00:36:11,360 --> 00:36:16,200
in with it, because oil and water do not mix.

531
00:36:16,300 --> 00:36:19,840
Inside the dryer, Vulcan also introduces recycled asphalt

532
00:36:19,940 --> 00:36:24,040
into the blend, saving on both rock and oil.

533
00:36:24,041 --> 00:36:26,540
This is recycled asphalt.

534
00:36:26,541 --> 00:36:29,540
This is one of the most recycled products by Wade

535
00:36:29,541 --> 00:36:31,540
in the United States.

536
00:36:31,541 --> 00:36:34,540
We use quite a bit of it here, very important part

537
00:36:34,541 --> 00:36:36,540
of our asphalt mixture.

538
00:36:36,541 --> 00:36:42,540
It includes the liquid oil that was previously used.

539
00:36:42,541 --> 00:36:45,040
Only now is the aggregate showered with fresh liquid

540
00:36:45,041 --> 00:36:49,540
asphalt from a refinery.

541
00:36:49,541 --> 00:36:53,540
After a brief but intensive mix, the process is complete.

542
00:36:53,541 --> 00:37:00,920
Okay, this is our final product being shipped out to the job site.

543
00:37:00,921 --> 00:37:05,540
As you can see by the size of the aggregate in it, it's a three-quarter inch mix.

544
00:37:05,541 --> 00:37:07,780
It will be put down in the pavement.

545
00:37:07,781 --> 00:37:11,880
We'll probably drive over it this afternoon sometime.

546
00:37:11,881 --> 00:37:16,220
An asphalt road, another oil product we take for granted.

547
00:37:16,221 --> 00:37:18,560
But there's a question to be answered.

548
00:37:18,561 --> 00:37:21,540
What happens when all the oil is gone?

549
00:37:23,540 --> 00:37:29,540
America's roads contain more than 19,000 square miles of

550
00:37:29,541 --> 00:37:33,540
asphalt, enough to pave over all of New Jersey, Connecticut,

551
00:37:33,541 --> 00:37:39,540
Delaware, and Rhode Island. Secrets of Oil will return on

552
00:37:39,541 --> 00:37:43,540
Modern Marvels, here on History.

553
00:37:47,540 --> 00:37:51,540
So what happens when it all runs out?

554
00:37:51,541 --> 00:37:55,480
No one knows the time or date, but there is an emerging consensus.

555
00:37:56,340 --> 00:37:58,980
As demand grows, supply will dwindle,

556
00:37:59,540 --> 00:38:03,640
until one day crude oil will disappear as a primary natural resource.

557
00:38:04,720 --> 00:38:06,600
And we'd better start to prepare.

558
00:38:07,200 --> 00:38:11,980
If you look at this objectively, you realize that this is a tremendous resource

559
00:38:11,981 --> 00:38:14,500
that perhaps has not been utilized properly.

560
00:38:14,920 --> 00:38:18,080
We depend on it, assuming that it will last forever.

561
00:38:18,081 --> 00:38:22,400
And these are finite resources. They never last forever.

562
00:38:23,600 --> 00:38:27,440
We're developing alternatives for almost every one of oil's fractions.

563
00:38:28,700 --> 00:38:31,520
And renewable energy supplies are on the move.

564
00:38:33,300 --> 00:38:37,380
Wind power, for example, now supplies 1% of America's energy needs,

565
00:38:38,460 --> 00:38:40,240
echoing oil's own early history.

566
00:38:41,860 --> 00:38:43,640
And it doesn't stop there.

567
00:38:44,600 --> 00:38:47,500
This driveway is paved not with traditional asphalt,

568
00:38:48,080 --> 00:38:50,540
but a bio-asphalt garnered from sugarcane.

569
00:38:52,620 --> 00:38:55,900
And while most synthetic motor oil still contains petroleum,

570
00:38:56,660 --> 00:39:00,000
the cost of fully synthesizing lubricants continues to drop.

571
00:39:03,140 --> 00:39:06,500
Virgin Atlantic Airlines recently flew the first jetliner,

572
00:39:06,680 --> 00:39:10,280
powered, in part, by a jet fuel created from algae.

573
00:39:15,160 --> 00:39:18,060
Photovoltaic solar cells like those atop this FedEx hub

574
00:39:18,061 --> 00:39:22,620
not only offer power today, but may well fuel the cars of tomorrow.

575
00:39:26,740 --> 00:39:34,460
And plastics that were once made from crude are now predominantly made from oil-based, yet cleaner-burning, natural gas.

576
00:39:36,620 --> 00:39:39,500
Plastics can also be made from corn and other crops.

577
00:39:41,680 --> 00:39:46,540
But new supplies of energy and feedstocks aren't the only area of research and invention.

578
00:39:46,541 --> 00:39:56,680
Now, can these alternative energy sources make up for, you know, the declining use of oil and gas or coal?

579
00:39:57,280 --> 00:40:00,980
That's a question that depends on how much technology we can develop.

580
00:40:01,400 --> 00:40:08,920
On the side of demand, on the other hand, you can make things a lot more efficient so that you can decrease the demand.

581
00:40:09,040 --> 00:40:10,900
And that's where recycling becomes an issue.

582
00:40:10,901 --> 00:40:17,900
Attacking demand by recycling what already exists is a familiar strategy in thousands

583
00:40:17,901 --> 00:40:20,419
of communities across the country.

584
00:40:20,420 --> 00:40:24,420
Look no further than plastic bottles, which regenerate

585
00:40:24,421 --> 00:40:28,082
into more than 20 million pounds of plastic every year.

586
00:40:28,083 --> 00:40:30,080
The challenge, however, is not

587
00:40:30,081 --> 00:40:37,360
in simple plastics like bottles, but more complicated products like TVs or computers,

588
00:40:37,361 --> 00:40:42,360
which contain many different kinds of plastics, all fused together.

589
00:40:42,361 --> 00:40:46,360
Until recently, the only answer was landfill.

590
00:40:46,361 --> 00:40:49,360
Enter the new science of recovery.

591
00:40:49,361 --> 00:40:52,360
A huge market is developing around recovering,

592
00:40:52,361 --> 00:40:57,360
and there are lots of folks out there hungry for plastics that can be recycled,

593
00:40:57,361 --> 00:40:59,360
but we've got to get them out of our trash.

594
00:40:59,361 --> 00:41:01,360
That's what we have to figure out ways to do,

595
00:41:01,361 --> 00:41:04,360
not just because we're more environmentally conscious,

596
00:41:04,361 --> 00:41:10,540
because the plastic itself has gone up in value literally two or three or four times.

597
00:41:12,180 --> 00:41:19,400
In 1994, Trip Allen founded MBA Polymers to recover the plastics bonded together in high-tech equipment.

598
00:41:21,920 --> 00:41:24,840
You take advantage of the properties that the plastics have.

599
00:41:25,000 --> 00:41:28,240
For example, density. Plastics have a variety of density.

600
00:41:28,880 --> 00:41:33,300
Polyethylene, coming from like a detergent bottle or a milk jug, is lighter than water.

601
00:41:33,301 --> 00:41:36,300
Polyester, PET, which is used in your soda bottle,

602
00:41:36,301 --> 00:41:37,300
is heavier than water.

603
00:41:37,301 --> 00:41:41,300
So you take those two, grind them up, shake them up,

604
00:41:41,301 --> 00:41:43,300
so you have just in a simple water solution,

605
00:41:43,301 --> 00:41:45,300
you see the separation.

606
00:41:45,301 --> 00:41:49,300
The polyethylene floats and the polyester, or PET, sinks.

607
00:41:49,301 --> 00:41:50,300
Now, it's not always that simple,

608
00:41:50,301 --> 00:41:53,300
and so you have other operations that you can use

609
00:41:53,301 --> 00:41:56,300
that will give you finer cuts.

610
00:41:56,301 --> 00:42:00,300
Today, computers contain 12 different types of plastic,

611
00:42:00,301 --> 00:42:02,300
all of which are recoverable.

612
00:42:02,301 --> 00:42:06,300
But what about a product that has 39 different plastics?

613
00:42:06,301 --> 00:42:09,300
You guessed it, the family car.

614
00:42:17,300 --> 00:42:20,300
This is what's left of the family car.

615
00:42:20,301 --> 00:42:24,300
When it gets shredded, they pull out the steel and a lot of

616
00:42:24,301 --> 00:42:27,300
the non-ferrous metals, and they leave behind a material that's

617
00:42:27,301 --> 00:42:29,300
called fluff, automotive fluff.

618
00:42:29,301 --> 00:42:34,420
automotive fluff. This has got everything from taillight housings to rocks to

619
00:42:34,421 --> 00:42:39,740
carpeting. It's a real mix of materials. These leftovers are soon sent to

620
00:42:39,741 --> 00:42:44,700
landfills. It doesn't stink as much as regular garbage so they use it to cover

621
00:42:44,701 --> 00:42:50,160
regular garbage. And that would seem to be the end of that.

622
00:42:50,161 --> 00:42:56,900
But like a prospector of old, Allen sees gold in those garbage hills. This truck

623
00:42:56,901 --> 00:43:03,460
uses a number of different technologies that are synthesized mostly from old mining industry ideas.

624
00:43:05,540 --> 00:43:10,580
California was founded around the gold rush and what is gold mining other than sorting one type of

625
00:43:10,581 --> 00:43:14,128
shiny yellow rock from a lot of other dingy brown ones.

626
00:43:14,129 --> 00:43:16,580
The process begins with the automotive

627
00:43:16,581 --> 00:43:21,614
fluff passing through a series of magnets to remove any small metal bits.

628
00:43:21,615 --> 00:43:22,580
The fluff then

629
00:43:22,581 --> 00:43:24,980
and enters a series of water cyclones,

630
00:43:24,981 --> 00:43:28,820
swirling tornadoes of H2O, that intensify the sinking

631
00:43:28,821 --> 00:43:30,800
or floating of the different plastics

632
00:43:30,801 --> 00:43:32,180
and other elements within the fluff.

633
00:43:34,680 --> 00:43:37,640
By sorting everything by how rapidly it sinks

634
00:43:37,641 --> 00:43:39,920
or how rapidly it floats, we can create

635
00:43:39,921 --> 00:43:42,860
a number of different products using just water.

636
00:43:42,861 --> 00:43:46,180
There's a lot of steel and non-ferrous metals,

637
00:43:46,181 --> 00:43:49,140
but there's also several different varieties of plastics

638
00:43:49,141 --> 00:43:53,020
in here, and those can go right back into consumer goods.

639
00:43:53,021 --> 00:43:56,320
Still only a prototype, the potential of this mobile water

640
00:43:56,321 --> 00:44:00,160
cyclone system is clear, and its insights may well be applied

641
00:44:00,161 --> 00:44:03,480
to still larger challenges, like the landfills themselves.

642
00:44:06,760 --> 00:44:10,180
But for now, it's a reminder that in a world wrestling

